  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on survey data from a single city, Beijing, which may limit their generalizability to cities with different urban forms, transit systems, or bike-sharing regulatory frameworks.",
      "what": "This study analyzed the factors influencing bike-sharing travel behavior in Beijing using structural equation modeling with four latent variables: behavioral attitude, subjective norm, perceived behavioral control, and shared bicycle satisfaction. The model was estimated and refined using AMOS software based on survey data collected from local residents, measuring satisfaction specifically through safety, convenience, and economy.",
      "why": "It establishes a structured, empirical relationship between user psychology and bike-sharing usage, demonstrating how subjective norms and perceived control interact with specific service attributes like safety and cost to drive travel choices."
    }
